WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:07.734
I'm headed to the American
Philosophical Society

00:00:07.734 --> 00:00:11.333
in Philadelphia to
meet with its librarian

00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:14.667
Patrick Spiro he
studies documents

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:17.233
dating back to the time
of the country's founding.

00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:24.100
What you're looking
at here is one

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.633
of the first printings of the
Declaration of Independence.

00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:29.834
The first section
is the Preamble.

00:00:29.834 --> 00:00:32.200
And this is where they
talk about life, liberty,

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.767
and the pursuit of happiness.

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:36.133
And the idea is that
individuals should

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:38.367
be free to do these
things, and government

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:41.567
is constituted to
protect those freedoms.

00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:45.000
And what the King has done
is broken that contract,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.400
broken that trust.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.667
And so they have to
be freed from the King

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:52.133
in order to be free
to do what they want.

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.700
Now, can you say that
this was the first time

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.867
a group of people
decided that they

00:00:56.867 --> 00:01:01.300
wanted to be free to do whatever
the heck they wanted to do?

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.900
Well, I think it's the
first time that it was

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.400
ever written in a official way.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.333
But this is not the only
version of the Declaration

00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:14.433
of Independence that survives.

00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:16.734
The other document I
want to show you is this.

00:01:16.734 --> 00:01:20.400
Thomas Jefferson's draft of the
Declaration of Independence.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.400
And you can see on the
side, there's these notes.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.433
Yeah?

00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:24.834
Once Congress got
their hands on this,

00:01:24.834 --> 00:01:26.633
they started changing
words, changing meanings.

00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:29.900
I think the most notable one
is, in that famous phrase,

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.367
that people are endowed with
certain unalienable rights,

00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:37.500
Jefferson originally wrote,
inherent and inalienable

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.767
rights.

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:41.500
Inherent rights, which
Jefferson used several times,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:46.734
means that all people are
born with these rights.

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:47.734
OK.

00:01:47.734 --> 00:01:50.400
So if these rights
are not inherent,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.066
then you're not
necessarily born with them?

00:01:54.066 --> 00:01:56.233
Only a few people
are born with them.

00:01:56.233 --> 00:01:59.734
And that applied
only to white society.

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:00.767
White, male society.

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:01.600
Yes.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.133
Yes.

